WEBVTT

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:07.968
One visual world model plans these behaviors from images.

00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:13.143
We use physical trajectories during training to improve the representations used

00:00:13.062 --> 00:00:14.805
for predicting action outcomes.

00:00:18.217 --> 00:00:20.722
The encoder and predictor learn together.

00:00:21.043 --> 00:00:26.447
Can physical grounding make the representation's dynamics forecastable beyond

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:27.935
that particular training pair?

00:00:28.242 --> 00:00:29.635
This is the question we study.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.295
We encode paired image and physical histories.

00:00:34.615 --> 00:00:39.070
The physical input describes geometry, pose, and the end effector.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.782
A shared predictor advances either history under action.

00:00:43.102 --> 00:00:48.545
Matching each prediction to both future representations couples visual and physical

00:00:48.477 --> 00:00:50.345
states through their evolution.

00:00:51.977 --> 00:00:55.332
After training, we remove the physical branch.

00:00:55.653 --> 00:01:00.645
The visual encoder and predictor retain the baseline's deployment architecture

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:01.782
and planning budget.

00:01:03.145 --> 00:01:07.087
Here, both models forecast identical stacking actions.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.625
After release, our model predicts the eraser remaining on its support, matching

00:01:12.558 --> 00:01:13.463
ground truth.

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:16.088
The baseline predicts it inside the block.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.237
These are decoded forecasts, not executed behaviors.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:26.770
Across six manipulation families, mean control success increases from fifty-three

00:01:26.703 --> 00:01:29.595
point six to seventy-eight point two percent.

00:01:29.927 --> 00:01:33.370
One model is trained across twenty-two configurations.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.282
Every family improves under the same planner and search budget.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:42.700
We freeze each visual encoder and fit a new predictor.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.725
Twenty-step relative drift drops from zero point three six one to zero point one

00:01:48.645 --> 00:01:49.550
zero four.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.612
The gain persists beyond the original predictor.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:59.025
Alignment improves control; cross-prediction further lowers drift.

00:01:59.345 --> 00:02:01.462
Sharing alone adds little.

00:02:01.782 --> 00:02:07.275
Separate single-task benchmarks also show consistently lower drift, with task-dependent

00:02:07.195 --> 00:02:08.237
control gains.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:14.657
Corresponding physical futures shape visual representations for prediction and

00:02:14.577 --> 00:02:15.258
control.

00:02:15.577 --> 00:02:18.483
The physical branch is needed only during training.
